      "text": "The objective function is expressed by the function $f=\\boxed{z=x^2+6x+5}+5y^2-4y+22-6$. The discriminant $\\Delta$ of this quadratic is $\\Delta=\\boxed{-20}$, hence the minimum value of $z$ is equal to $\\boxed{z=34}$. To find the minimum value of the function \\( f(x, y) = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms.\n\nFirst, consider the \\(x\\) terms: \n\\[ x^2 + 6x \\]\nWe complete the square by adding and subtracting 9:\n\\[ x^2 + 6x = (x + 3)^2 - 9 \\]\n\nNext, consider the \\(y\\) terms: \n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y \\]\nWe complete the square by adding and subtracting 2:\n\\[ 2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y - 1)^2 - 1) = 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 \\]\n\nNow substitute these completed squares back into the original function:\n\\[ f(x, y) = (x + 3)^2 - 9 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 2 + 22 \\]\nCombine the constants:\n\\[ f(x, y) = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 - 9 - 2 + 22 \\]\n\\[ f(x, y) = (x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2 + 11 \\]\n\nSince \\((x + 3)^2\\) and \\((y - 1)^2\\) are both non-negative (i.e., they are always greater than or equal to zero), the minimum value of the expression \\((x + 3)^2 + 2(y - 1)^2\\) is 0, which occurs when both \\(x + 3 = 0\\) and \\(y - 1 = 0\\), i.e., \\(x = -3\\) and \\(y = 1\\).\n\nThus, the minimum value of \\(f(x, y)\\) is:\n\\[ f(x, y) = 0 + 0 + 11 = 11 \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(z\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).",
