      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the minimum value of \\( z = x^2 + 2y^2 + 6x - 4y + 22 \\), we can apply the method of completing the square. Specifically, we will rewrite the expression in a form that clearly shows the minimum value due to symmetry.\n\nFirst, consider the first three terms in the expression:\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x = (x^2 + 6x + 9 - 9) = (x+3)^2 - 9\n\\]\nThis form allows us to complete the square as follows:\n\\[\nx^2 + 6x = (x+3)^2 - 9\n\\]\nNext, we concentrate on the linear terms. The quadratic form \\( 2y^2 - 4y \\) can be factored as:\n\\[\n2y^2 - 4y = 2(y^2 - 2y) = 2((y-1)^2 - 1) = 2(y-1)^2 - 2\n\\]\nSo, the expression \\( z \\) can be rewritten inside the function we are trying to minimize:\n\\[\nz = 2(x+3)^2 - 9 + 2(y-1)^2 - 2 + 22\n\\]\nSimplifying the constants:\n\\[\nz = 2(x+3)^2 + 2(y-1)^2 + 9\n\\]\nThis shows that \\( z \\) is a sum of squares, \\( 2(x+3)^2 \\) and \\( 2(y-1)^2 \\). Sum of two squares is always non-negative and the minimum value is 0. We can therefore assert the minimum value of \\( z \\) when both squares are zero:\n\\[\n2(x+3)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2(y-1)^2 = 0\n\\]\nThis happens when:\n\\[\n(x+3)^2 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = -3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y-1)^2 = 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad y = 1\n\\]\nSubstituting these values of \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) into the original expression for \\( z \\):\n\\[\nz = (x+3)^2 + (y-1)^2 + 22\n\\]\nWhen \\( x = -3 \\) and \\( y = 1 \\):\n\\[\nz = (-3 + 3)^2 + (1-1)^2 + 22 = 0 + 0 + 22 = 22\n\\]\nThus, the minimum value of \\( z \\) is \\(\\boxed{22}\\)",
